window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"Module 1: What AI is","u":"modules/what-is-ai/","k":"Module","d":"What artificial intelligence is, how it learns, and where lawyers already meet it.","x":"AI is software that learns patterns from examples. Nobody writes all its rules by hand. Courts have accepted some AI for years, such as technology-assisted review in e-discovery. AI has no duties and cannot be disciplined. The lawyer stays responsible for the work."},{"t":"AI in everyday legal work","u":"lessons/ai-in-legal-work/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 1: What AI is","d":"AI stands for artificial intelligence. Lawyers and law students already use it, often without noticing.","x":"You already use AI When your email moves junk mail out of your inbox, that is AI. When a legal research site suggests other cases you may want to read, that is often AI too. So is the software in a big lawsuit that sorts millions of emails into “likely relevant” and “likely not relevant.” All of these do the same kind of job. A computer makes a prediction , which is a careful guess. It bases that guess on patterns it found in lots of examples. Definition Artificial intelligence (AI) is a computer system that does jobs that usually need a person’s thinking. Sorting documents, understanding speech and writing a first draft are all examples. Courts have accepted AI for years In 2012, a federal judge in New York approved a method called predictive coding in the case Da Silva Moore v. Publicis Groupe . It is known as the first time a court approved this method. Lawyers marked a sample of documents as relevant or not. The software learned from those choices and ranked the rest. Today this is often called technology-assisted review , or TAR. What AI is not Films often show AI as a robot that thinks like a person. Real AI has no feelings or opinions, and it does not know that it exists. It owes no duty to a client, and no bar can discipline it. It is a machine that is good at finding patterns. You stay responsible for the work. Example A review tool does not “know” that an email is about a price-fixing deal. It learned which words and patterns showed up in the emails lawyers marked as relevant. Then it looks for those patterns in new emails. Why AI is suddenly everywhere People have been building AI since the 1950s. It got much better in the last 15 years. The internet gave computers billions of pages to learn from. Computer chips became much faster. Researchers also found better ways for computers to learn from examples. The most important of these is called deep learning . You will meet it in the lesson Types of AI . Most AI today is machine learning . A machine learning model is a math formula with millions or even trillions of settings, called parameters . Training adjusts those settings until the formula fits the examples. A document review tool is a classifier , which means it sorts things into groups. It learns from documents that lawyers have already marked as relevant or not relevant. This is called supervised learning . For each new document, it gives a score for how likely the document is to be relevant. Which of these uses AI? Da Silva Moore v. Publicis Groupe, 287 F.R.D. 182 (S.D.N.Y. 2012)"},{"t":"How AI learns","u":"lessons/how-ai-learns/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 1: What AI is","d":"Nobody gives AI a full set of rules. It learns from examples, much like a new associate learns from marked-up drafts.","x":"Learning from examples A normal computer program follows rules that a person writes. For example, “If a deadline falls on a Saturday, move it to Monday.” Someone has to think of every rule ahead of time. AI works another way. You give it thousands of examples instead of rules. You might show it 5,000 contracts where a lawyer has marked each clause that limits liability. The AI then works out for itself what a limitation-of-liability clause tends to look like. Comparison Nobody gave you a rule book for spotting a weak argument. You read many briefs, saw what worked and got feedback. Over time you learned the patterns. AI learns in a similar way, but from far more examples and with no real understanding. Training data The examples an AI learns from are called training data . Their quality matters a lot. An AI that learned mostly from federal court opinions may be weaker on state law or on the law of other countries. An AI that learned from old contracts may miss newer clauses. This is also how AI can become biased , which means unfair to some groups of people. If the examples are unfair or one-sided, the AI learns that too. You will see real legal examples in the lesson When AI is unfair . Practice, millions of times During training , the AI makes a guess and checks whether it was right. Then it changes itself a tiny bit and tries again. It does this millions of times. Each small fix makes the next guess a little better. Nobody writes the rules. They grow out of all that practice. Example In a large lawsuit, a lawyer reviews a few thousand documents and marks each one relevant or not. The review tool learns from those marks. It then ranks the other million documents, so people read the most likely relevant ones first. Training tries to make a number called the loss as small as possible. The loss measures how wrong the model’s guesses are. A method called gradient descent works out which way to nudge each setting to lower the loss, using a step called backpropagation. It then nudges all the settings a little, one small batch of examples at a time. Engineers keep some examples aside for testing. This shows whether the model learned the pattern or only memorized the answers, which is called overfitting . In e-discovery, the same idea appears as measuring recall (how many relevant documents were found) and precision (how many of the documents found were relevant) on a test sample. What is training data? "},{"t":"Types of AI","u":"lessons/types-of-ai/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 1: What AI is","d":"You will hear names like machine learning, deep learning and generative AI. They fit inside each other like boxes.","x":"One picture explains it These names do not compete with each other. Each one sits inside the one before it. Each box is a more specific kind of the box around it. Artificial intelligence This is the big idea. Computers do jobs that seem to need human thinking. Machine learning This is the main way people build AI today. The computer learns patterns from examples, as you saw in the last lesson. Technology-assisted review in e-discovery, which is finding and reviewing electronic evidence for a lawsuit, is machine learning. When people say “AI” today, they usually mean machine learning. Deep learning Deep learning is a strong kind of machine learning. Its design was loosely inspired by the brain. It uses neural networks , which are many layers of simple math stacked on top of each other. These layers can find complicated patterns. Deep learning runs speech-to-text transcription, translation and chatbots. Generative AI Generative AI is deep learning that makes new things. It can draft a letter, sum up a deposition (a witness’s recorded testimony before trial), translate a contract or write computer code. Older AI mostly sorted or labeled things. This difference matters in law. A tool that sorts documents can miss some. A tool that writes can invent things that are not true. Narrow AI and general AI Every AI you can use today is “narrow.” It is good at the jobs it was trained for and nothing else. Some companies are trying to build “general AI,” also called AGI. That would be a machine that can do any thinking task as well as a person. Experts disagree about how close anyone is to building it. A neural network is a stack of layers. Each layer multiplies its inputs by a grid of numbers and then applies a simple bend, called a non-linear function. “Deep” means there are many layers. Generative models learn how likely different outputs are and then pick from those chances. Language models do this one word piece at a time. Image models called diffusion models learn to undo added noise. Older e-discovery tools often used simpler machine learning methods, such as logistic regression or support vector machines, which can work well with less data. How does generative AI fit with machine learning? "},{"t":"Module 2: Generative AI","u":"modules/generative-ai/","k":"Module","d":"AI that drafts, sums up and makes pictures and voices. What it means for evidence, copyright and fraud.","x":"Generative AI makes new text, pictures, sound and video. A fast draft is only useful once someone checks it. AI can make fake photos, voices and videos that look real. Ask where any file came from before relying on it. Courts are still deciding who owns AI-made work and what AI may train on. Confirm payment changes by calling a number you already have."},{"t":"What generative AI makes","u":"lessons/what-generative-ai-makes/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 2: Generative AI","d":"Generative AI can write, sum up, translate, draw and speak. Some of these uses save time in legal work, and some create new risks.","x":"AI that makes things Older AI mostly checked or sorted things. It answered questions like “Is this email junk?” or “Is this document relevant?” Generative AI makes things. You describe what you want, and it creates something new. Definition Generative AI creates new content. It does this using patterns it learned from a huge amount of content made by people. What it can make Text First drafts of letters and emails, summaries of long documents, translations and checklists. Examples are ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and the AI tools inside legal research services. Speech to text Written transcripts of recorded meetings and calls, often with a short summary. Pictures and video Images and short clips made from a description. Examples are Midjourney, Adobe Firefly, Google Veo and OpenAI Sora. Voices Realistic speech in many voices. These tools can also copy a real person’s voice, which matters for fraud and evidence. Code Working computer programs from requests in plain English, such as a small script that renames a folder of exhibits. Why it matters for legal work A first draft that once took an hour can now take a minute. That saves time on routine writing. The draft still needs a lawyer’s judgment. It may leave out a key fact, use the wrong law or state something false. In legal work, a fast draft is only useful once someone has checked it. Different kinds of media have used different designs. Text and code mostly use Transformers . Images, sound and video often use diffusion or a related method called flow matching. More and more, one multimodal model handles every kind of media by turning it all into small pieces called tokens. The output involves some chance, so the same request can give different results. Settings such as temperature and the random seed control how much the results vary. For legal drafting, that variation is a reason to review every version you get. Which of these is generative AI? "},{"t":"How AI makes pictures and video","u":"lessons/how-ai-makes-pictures/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 2: Generative AI","d":"AI can now make photos and videos that look real. Knowing how it works helps you judge evidence.","x":"From static to a picture Most picture tools use a method called diffusion . In training, the AI takes real pictures and adds random dots, like the static on an old TV. It keeps adding dots until nothing is left but static. Then it practices the opposite. It turns static back into a clear picture, a little at a time. Comparison Picture a film of a sandcastle washing away in the waves, one frame at a time. Suppose you watched that film millions of times. In the end, you could play it backwards and build a castle out of loose sand. Picture AI learned to build pictures out of static in a similar way. Where your words come in When you type a description, another AI turns your words into directions. The picture AI then cleans up the static step by step, moving closer to a picture that matches your words. Video tools do the same thing across many frames. Why this matters for evidence The result can look exactly like a real photo, voice recording or video. A deepfake is a fake photo, voice or video of a real person, made with AI. Deepfakes can appear in court as fake evidence. A party may also claim that real evidence is a deepfake, to cast doubt on it. What careful lawyers do They ask where a file came from and who has handled it. They look for the original file and its details, such as when and on what device it was made. They check for a content label that says how the file was made. When the stakes are high, they hire an expert to examine the file. Most picture models run diffusion in a squeezed-down version of the image, called a latent space , instead of on every pixel. A text encoder turns your words into lists of numbers called embeddings . At each clean-up step, those numbers steer the picture. A standard called C2PA can attach signed “content credentials” to a file to record how it was made and edited. Some companies also hide invisible watermarks in AI-made media. Tools that claim to detect AI pictures are unreliable, so treat their answers as one clue, never as proof. How does a diffusion picture tool make an image? "},{"t":"Copyright, deepfakes and scams","u":"lessons/copyright-and-deepfakes/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 2: Generative AI","d":"Courts are still working out who owns what AI makes and what AI companies may train on. Fake voices and videos add new risks.","x":"Who owns what AI makes In the United States, copyright protects work made by people. In March 2025, a federal appeals court said so plainly in Thaler v. Perlmutter . An AI system cannot be the author of a work under the Copyright Act. The court did not decide how much a person must add to an AI-made work for it to be protected. Other countries have their own rules. What AI companies may train on Many lawsuits ask whether AI companies may train their systems on other people’s work without permission. One early ruling came from inside the legal world. In February 2025, a federal judge in Delaware ruled in Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence . Ross had used Westlaw headnotes, the short case summaries Westlaw editors write, to build a competing legal research tool. The judge rejected Ross’s fair use defense, which allows some uses of copyrighted work without permission. Other cases are still being decided, so the law here is unsettled. Deepfakes and scams AI can copy someone’s voice from a short recording. Law firms handle client money and wire transfers, which makes them targets. A caller might sound exactly like a partner or a client. Protect client money Never move money because of a call, voice message or email alone. Confirm by calling back on a number you already have on file. Use a second person to approve any change to payment details. Say when you used AI Many courts, schools and employers now have rules about using AI and saying when you did. Check the rules that apply to you before you use it for coursework, a court filing or client work. In Thomson Reuters v. Ross Intelligence , the court found infringement of 2,243 Westlaw headnotes and weighed the market effect most heavily, because Ross was building a competing product. The ruling involved a non-generative search tool, so its reach to generative AI training is debated. Many other cases against AI companies are still in progress, and some have reached different results on fair use. Check the current state of the law in your jurisdiction before you advise anyone. In Thaler v. Perlmutter (2025), what did the federal appeals court decide? Thaler v. Perlmutter, No. 23-5233 (D.C. Cir. Mar. 18, 2025) Thomson Reuters Enter. Ctr. GmbH v. Ross Intelligence Inc., No. 1:20-cv-613-SB, mem. op. (D. Del. Feb. 11, 2025)"},{"t":"Module 3: Chatbots and AI models","u":"modules/chatbots/","k":"Module","d":"How chatbots work, how to ask good legal questions, why they invent cases, and how to pick a tool.","x":"A language model writes by guessing the next word, so it can produce cases that sound real but are not. Good prompts give your role, the place, the task and the format, and leave client details out. Lawyers have been sanctioned for filing fake AI citations. Read every case you cite. Legal research tools are better than general chatbots for finding authority, but they still make mistakes."},{"t":"How chatbots work","u":"lessons/how-chatbots-work/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 3: Chatbots and AI models","d":"ChatGPT, Claude and Gemini run on large language models. A language model writes by guessing the next word, again and again.","x":"Guessing the next word A language model does one main job. It looks at some text and guesses which word comes next. Then it adds that word and guesses again. In training, it practices this billions of times on a huge amount of writing. After all that practice, it gets good at writing text that reads well. You can try it below. The bars show how likely the model thinks each next word is. Why this matters for lawyers A chatbot writes what sounds likely. A case name that sounds right, with a citation in the right format, is exactly the kind of thing it can produce, whether or not the case exists. The lesson When chatbots get it wrong shows what happened when lawyers forgot this. What “large” means A model stores what it learned as billions of numbers that training adjusts. These numbers are called parameters . The biggest models have hundreds of billions of them or more. More parameters usually means more knowledge and skill. It also costs more to run. What they learn from Language models learn from books, websites, computer code and other writing, which can include court opinions and statutes that are online. That is why a chatbot can explain a legal idea well. It does not mean it knows the current law where you practice. How much they understand Researchers still argue about how much these models understand. For you, one point matters most. Usually the text is helpful, and sometimes it is wrong in ways that are hard to spot. Your own judgment still decides what goes to a client or a court. Chat models are a kind of Transformer . Each layer uses a step called self-attention to let every word piece look at the others, then passes the result through a small network. The last layer gives a probability for every possible next piece. A model’s list of pieces, its vocabulary, usually holds about 100,000 to 200,000 of them. The model writes one piece at a time. Legal research tools add a search step before the model writes, which is covered in the lesson Building with AI . What does a language model do, at the most basic level? Ashish Vaswani et al., Attention Is All You Need, 30 Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems (2017)"},{"t":"Asking good legal questions","u":"lessons/asking-good-questions/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 3: Chatbots and AI models","d":"The way you ask changes the answer you get. A clear, detailed prompt gets a much more useful reply.","x":"Your words matter The message you type is called a prompt . A vague prompt gets a vague answer. A specific prompt gets a specific, useful answer. Vague prompt “Tell me about landlord-tenant law.” Usual answer A general overview that fits no state in particular and no real problem. Specific prompt “I’m a paralegal in Illinois. Make a checklist of the facts to collect from a tenant who says the landlord kept the security deposit. Put it in a table, and list the statute I should read to confirm the rules.” Usual answer A practical checklist you can use, plus a pointer to the law you must check yourself. What to put in a good prompt Who you are. Say your role, such as student, paralegal or associate, and what you already know. Where. Name the country, state or court. Law differs from place to place. The task. Say exactly what you want, such as a checklist, an outline or a plain-language summary. The shape of the answer. Ask for a table, bullet points or a set length. Honesty. Ask it to say what it is unsure about and which sources you should check. Leave client details out Describe the problem with made-up names and only the facts the AI needs. Do not paste in client names, private documents or anything covered by your duty of confidentiality, unless your employer has approved the tool for that use. The lesson Confidentiality and privilege explains why. Keep the conversation going Your first prompt does not need to be perfect. Reply with things like “make it shorter,” “write it for a client with no legal training” or “what facts would change your answer?” Chatbots remember the conversation, so improving the answer bit by bit is normal. What chatbots do well and less well Chatbots are good at explaining ideas, making outlines and checklists, and rewriting text for a different reader. They are also good at summing up a long document you give them. They are weak at exact legal sources. They can invent cases and quotes, mix up states and miss recent changes in the law. Behind the chat window, your message is joined to the earlier conversation and to hidden instructions from the company, called the system prompt . Methods that help with legal tasks include giving a clear role and task, showing an example of the output you want (called few-shot prompting), asking the model to list its assumptions, asking for a fixed format, and splitting a big job, such as a research memo, into smaller steps. Asking the model to cite sources does not make the sources real. You still have to check each one. Which prompt is most likely to get a useful answer? "},{"t":"When chatbots get it wrong","u":"lessons/when-chatbots-get-it-wrong/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 3: Chatbots and AI models","d":"Chatbots can invent cases, quotes and facts and still sound sure of themselves. Courts have fined and disciplined lawyers for trusting them.","x":"What a “hallucination” is When an AI states something false as if it were a fact, people call it a hallucination . It might invent a case, put a real case in the wrong court or make up a quote. This happens because the AI writes the words that sound most likely, and sometimes the most likely-sounding words are wrong. Real cases where it went wrong Mata v. Avianca, Inc. , 678 F. Supp. 3d 443 (S.D.N.Y. 2023) Lawyers filed a brief in federal court that cited cases ChatGPT had made up. When the court could not find the cases, the lawyers did not come clean right away. In June 2023 the judge fined the lawyers and their firm $5,000. Park v. Kim , 91 F.4th 610 (2d Cir. 2024) A lawyer cited a state court decision that did not exist. She said she had used ChatGPT to find it and did not check it. The federal appeals court referred her for possible discipline. It said lawyers must read the authorities they rely on to confirm they exist and support the point. United States v. Cohen , No. 18-cr-602 (S.D.N.Y. 2024) Michael Cohen, a former lawyer, gave his own lawyer case citations he had found with Google’s Bard chatbot. He thought it was a kind of search engine. The cases were fake and ended up in a court filing. The judge did not impose sanctions but called the lawyer’s conduct negligent. Legal research tools make mistakes too Tools built for lawyers search real legal databases before they answer, so they invent less. They still make mistakes. A Stanford study tested leading legal research tools in 2024 and was published in a law journal in 2025. It found that they gave false or misleading answers between 17 and 33 percent of the time. How to protect yourself Read every case you cite. Pull it from a trusted legal database or the court’s website, and make sure it says what you claim. Check that it is still good law, meaning no later court has overturned or weakened it. Use a citator service such as Shepard’s or KeyCite. Check every quote and page number against the original. Use AI for first drafts and ideas. Then fill in the law yourself, from sources you have read. How to treat AI research Treat AI research like notes from a new intern who has read a lot but never been to law school. Some of the leads will help. Check every one before you rely on it. Hallucinations happen because training rewards text that sounds likely, and sounding likely is different from being true. Legal research tools reduce this by first searching a database of real cases and then asking the model to answer from what it found. This is called retrieval-augmented generation, or RAG. It helps, but it does not solve the problem. The Stanford study found that the legal tools it tested gave false or misleading answers between 17 and 33 percent of the time, including answers that cited real cases for points those cases do not make. What did the court do in Mata v. Avianca (2023)? Mata v. Avianca, Inc., 678 F. Supp. 3d 443 (S.D.N.Y. 2023) (opinion and order on sanctions) Park v. Kim, 91 F.4th 610 (2d Cir. 2024) United States v. Cohen, No. 18-cr-602 (JMF), opinion and order (S.D.N.Y. Mar. 20, 2024) Varun Magesh et al., Hallucination-Free? Assessing the Reliability of Leading AI Legal Research Tools, 22 J. Empirical Legal Stud. 216 (2025) Patrick Lewis et al., Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks, 33 Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems 9459 (2020)"},{"t":"Choosing an AI model","u":"lessons/choosing-an-ai-model/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 3: Chatbots and AI models","d":"General chatbots and legal research tools each offer several models. They differ in size, speed, cost and how they handle your data.","x":"The app and the model The app is what you use, such as ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or a legal research service. The model is the AI system inside the app that does the work. Most apps let you switch between several models. Companies release new ones every few months, which is why the names keep changing. General chatbots and legal tools General chatbots ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Copilot. Good for explaining ideas, outlining, rewriting and summing up documents you give them. Unreliable for finding legal authority on their own. Legal research tools AI features inside services such as Lexis+ with Protégé and Westlaw, and other legal AI products. They search real legal databases before answering and link to sources. They still make mistakes. Practice tools Tools for contract review, e-discovery and drafting. They are built for one kind of legal task. The lesson Legal AI tools covers them. Large, medium and small models Most companies offer models in different sizes. Large models are the smartest and slowest, and they are best for long documents and hard problems. Small models are fast and cheap, and they are fine for quick rewrites. In Anthropic’s Claude family, Haiku is small, Sonnet is medium and Opus is large. Words like “mini,” “flash” or “lite” in a name usually mean a smaller, faster model. Thinking models Some models take extra time to work through a problem step by step before they answer. Some apps call this a “thinking” or “reasoning” setting. These models are slower and much better at multi-step problems, such as working out a deadline or comparing two contract versions. Other ways tools differ How much they can read at once. This is called the context window . A bigger one can handle a long contract or a deposition transcript. How they treat your data. Business and enterprise plans often promise not to train on your data. Free consumer plans may not. This matters for client confidentiality. Open models. Some models, such as Meta’s Llama, can run on your organization’s own computers, so data never leaves them. Which tool to use for client work For work involving clients, the right tool is usually the one your employer has approved and set up. The AI tools page lists common general and legal tools. Compare models on a set of your own test questions with known answers. Public rankings help less, because many models now score near the top. Useful things to compare are quality on your task, speed, price, how much text the model can read at once, and how the company handles your data. For legal work, also compare how often the tool’s citations are real and support the point. Test the tool on the kind of work you do, in your own jurisdiction, before you rely on it. Which tool fits best if you need current case law with real citations? "},{"t":"Module 4: AI in legal work","u":"modules/using-ai/","k":"Module","d":"Using AI for legal writing, for law school and at a legal job, within the rules.","x":"Let AI write a first draft, then check every fact and legal point and rewrite it in your own words. In law school, follow your school’s rules and use AI to test yourself, not to skip the reading. At work, use only approved tools for client information, bill honestly and supervise others’ AI use."},{"t":"AI for legal writing","u":"lessons/ai-for-legal-writing/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 4: AI in legal work","d":"AI can help you write faster and more clearly. The legal judgment, and the responsibility, stay with you.","x":"Where AI helps The hardest part of writing is often getting started. AI is good at writing a first draft that you can then fix. It is also good at shortening, reordering and changing the tone of text you already wrote. Plain-language versions. Turn a dense paragraph into something a client can follow. First drafts of routine letters. Cover notes for engagement letters (the letters that set out the terms of hiring a lawyer), scheduling emails and status updates. Editing. Ask it to “cut this by a third,” “fix the grammar” or “make the headings parallel.” Checklists and outlines. Turn your notes into an outline for a memo or a list of documents to request. Sample writing prompt “Write a short email to a client explaining that the hearing has moved from March 3 to March 17 and that they do not need to do anything yet. Use a warm, plain tone and no legal jargon. Use the name Jordan.” Then you replace the made-up name and check every date before sending. Where it can hurt you AI drafts can state the law wrongly, leave out a key fact or invent a quote. They can also sound confident about things you never said. For anything that goes to a client, an opposing party or a court, you must check every fact, date and legal point. Keep your own voice AI writing often sounds bland, like it could be about any case. Clients and judges notice. Rewrite the draft in your own words. Add the facts and reasons that only you know. Cut anything you would not say yourself. To get the same style across many documents, put your style guide and a few approved examples in the system prompt. Ask for a fixed structure, such as headings for facts, issue, rule and next steps. A lower temperature setting makes the wording more consistent from one try to the next. Some firms keep a library of approved clauses and have the system look up and reuse them, which is usually more reliable than retraining a model, called fine-tuning . What is the safest way to use AI for a client letter? "},{"t":"AI for law school and study","u":"lessons/ai-for-law-school/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 4: AI in legal work","d":"AI can be a patient study partner at any hour. Used well, it helps you learn the law instead of skipping the learning.","x":"Check your school’s rules first Law schools and professors set their own rules on AI. Some allow it for study but ban it on exams and graded writing. Some require you to say when you used it. Breaking these rules can be an honor code violation, which can follow you into the bar’s character and fitness review. Read your school’s policy and each syllabus. Good ways to study with AI Explain a rule at your level. Say what you already know and what confuses you. Teach it back. Explain a rule in your own words, then ask the AI to point out mistakes and gaps. Practice with hypotheticals. Ask for short made-up fact patterns and test yourself. Turn your own notes into flash cards or a quiz. Sample study prompt “I’m a first-year student. I understand that a contract needs an offer and acceptance, but I’m confused about the mailbox rule. Explain it with one everyday example. Then ask me two questions to check I understand.” Where it can mislead you AI can state a rule from the wrong state, mix up the majority and minority rules, or summarize a case wrongly. A case summary is not the case. Read the assigned cases and check AI explanations against your casebook, your professor’s materials or a trusted study aid. Study tools made for your own files Some tools answer questions using only the documents you upload, such as your class notes. One example is Google’s Gemini Notebook, which was called NotebookLM until July 2026. Only upload material you are allowed to share. Study tools that answer from your own files cut your uploads into short passages. They turn each passage into numbers that capture its meaning, called embeddings . When you ask a question, they find the closest passages and answer from them, with links back to the source. That is why they make up less about your material. Research supports two study methods most strongly. One is retrieval practice , which means being quizzed instead of rereading. The other is spaced repetition , which means reviewing at growing gaps of time. What does “teach it back” mean? John Dunlosky et al., Improving Students’ Learning With Effective Learning Techniques, 14 Psychol. Sci. Pub. Int. 4 (2013)"},{"t":"AI at your legal job","u":"lessons/ai-at-work/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 4: AI in legal work","d":"Firms, courts and legal departments now use AI for routine tasks. Your employer’s rules decide which tools you may use and how.","x":"Common uses in legal work Summaries. Turn a long deposition, contract or email chain into the main points, then check them against the source. Timelines. Pull dates and events from documents into a table. First-pass review. Flag clauses in a stack of contracts, such as terms that apply if a company is sold or that renew a contract automatically. Drafting from templates. Fill in routine documents from your firm’s approved forms. Getting ready for meetings. List questions to ask a client or witness. Use only approved tools Use only the AI tools your employer has approved for client work. Never put client names, privileged documents or confidential facts into a public chatbot. If you are not sure whether a tool is approved, ask before you use it. Billing and honesty AI can make some tasks much faster. ABA Formal Opinion 512, issued in July 2024, says lawyers who bill by the hour may only bill for the time they actually spent. Lawyers should not charge clients for time spent learning a general AI tool. Firms should tell clients how AI costs will be billed. Supervising others Partners and supervising lawyers must make sure the people they supervise use AI properly. That includes associates, paralegals, staff and outside vendors. That means clear policies, training and checking the work. Staying valuable AI handles more of the routine drafting and review. Judgment, client counseling, negotiation, advocacy and deep knowledge of an area of law become more important. Enterprise AI plans usually add protections such as a promise not to train on your data, short or zero data retention, single sign-on, audit logs and admin controls. If your organization connects AI to its document system, the connection should respect existing access rights, so the AI only shows each person the matters they are allowed to see. Ethical walls between matters must still work. Keep a record of how AI was used on a matter, in case a client or court asks. What should you check before you use an AI tool on client work? ABA Comm. on Ethics & Pro. Resp., Formal Op. 512, Generative Artificial Intelligence Tools (July 29, 2024)"},{"t":"Module 5: Fairness, privacy and legal jobs","u":"modules/safety-and-fairness/","k":"Module","d":"Bias in AI used for bail, sentencing and hiring, what happens to what you type, and how legal jobs are changing.","x":"AI can repeat unfair patterns. Courts and lawmakers have set limits on tools like risk scores and hiring screens. AI chats may be saved, read or handed over. A court has held a client’s own AI chats were not privileged. Routine review and drafting are changing most. Judgment, advocacy and client care matter more."},{"t":"When AI is unfair","u":"lessons/when-ai-is-unfair/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 5: Fairness, privacy and legal jobs","d":"AI can treat some people unfairly even when nobody meant it to. In law, that can affect bail, sentencing and hiring.","x":"Where bias comes from AI learns from data made by people. That data reflects our history, including the unfair parts. If an AI learns from one-sided examples, it picks up the same slant. Then it repeats that slant for many people at once. Risk scores in criminal cases Some courts use software that scores how likely a defendant is to commit another crime. One tool is called COMPAS. In 2016, an analysis by the news group ProPublica found that Black defendants were more often wrongly labeled high risk than white defendants. The tool’s maker disputed it, and the Wisconsin Supreme Court described both sides in the case below. A 2018 study in the journal Science Advances found that COMPAS was no better at predicting who would commit another crime than people with no criminal justice training. State v. Loomis , 2016 WI 68, 881 N.W.2d 749 A defendant argued that using a COMPAS score at his sentencing violated his right to due process, which means fair legal procedures. The Wisconsin Supreme Court disagreed, but it set limits. Judges may consider the score as one factor, not the deciding one. They must also get written warnings about the tool’s limits. The U.S. Supreme Court declined to hear the case. Hiring tools Employers use AI to screen résumés and rank candidates. If a tool learned from a company’s past hiring, it can copy past unfairness. New York City’s Local Law 144 requires employers that use these tools for hiring or promotion to have a yearly independent bias audit and to publish a summary of the results. The city began enforcing it in July 2023. Why it matters for lawyers AI now plays a part in decisions about bail, sentencing, jobs, loans and benefits. Lawyers may need to question how a tool works, ask for its testing results and argue about whether it treated their client fairly. Laws such as the European Union’s AI Act require extra checks for these high-risk uses. Researchers have several exact definitions of fairness. One is equal accuracy for every group. Another is equal rates of false alarms, such as being wrongly labeled high risk. A third is that a given score should mean the same thing for every group. These definitions usually cannot all be true at once, which is part of why COMPAS was so hotly debated. A bias audit breaks a tool’s results down by group, for example by comparing selection rates by sex and race, to spot gaps. In State v. Loomis (2016), what did the Wisconsin Supreme Court decide about COMPAS risk scores? State v. Loomis, 2016 WI 68, 371 Wis. 2d 235, 881 N.W.2d 749 Julia Dressel & Hany Farid, The Accuracy, Fairness, and Limits of Predicting Recidivism, 4 Sci. Advances eaao5580 (2018) N.Y.C. Local Law 144 of 2021, Automated Employment Decision Tools (Dep’t of Consumer & Worker Prot.)"},{"t":"Privacy and your data","u":"lessons/your-privacy/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 5: Fairness, privacy and legal jobs","d":"What you type into an AI tool may be saved, read or used for training. That matters for you and even more for your clients.","x":"Your chats may be saved and used What you type into a chatbot may be saved. Depending on the app and your settings, staff may read it to check for safety problems. It may also be used to train future AI. A company may have to hand chats over to the government or in a lawsuit. Check your settings Open the privacy settings in each AI app you use. Most apps let you stop your chats from being used for training. Many also let you start a temporary chat that is not saved, and delete your history. A court ruling on AI chats and privilege In February 2026, a federal judge in New York ruled in United States v. Heppner . The defendant had used an AI chatbot on his own to work through his legal situation, and later shared the results with his lawyers. The judge held that those chats were not protected by attorney-client privilege , the rule that keeps private talks between a client and lawyer secret. The chatbot is not a lawyer, and the company’s privacy policy allowed it to share data. The chats were not work product either. That rule protects materials prepared by or for a lawyer for a lawsuit, and no lawyer directed these chats. The ruling did not decide what happens when a lawyer directs the AI use or uses a closed business tool. Other courts may see it differently. It is a strong reason to warn clients that their own AI chats about their case may be seen by the other side. Never share these with a public chatbot Client names, case details or privileged documents. Passwords, PINs or security codes. Bank, card or government ID numbers. Anyone else’s private information without permission. Work and school accounts Paid business plans usually protect data better. For example, they often promise not to train AI on it. If your workplace gives you one of these accounts, use it for work, and follow its rules. Check whether a provider offers zero data retention , which means it does not keep what you send, and whether the contract promises not to train on your data. Check where the data is stored and who at the company can see it. For highly sensitive matters, options include removing names and identifying details before sending, running an open model on your organization’s own computers, or a private setup from a cloud provider. Prompts may also be saved in your own systems’ logs, which can be subject to discovery. In United States v. Heppner (2026), what did the court decide about a defendant’s own chats with an AI tool? United States v. Heppner, No. 25-cr-503 (JSR), ECF No. 27 (S.D.N.Y. Feb. 17, 2026) (scanned opinion) Andrea Roth & Rebecca Wexler, AI Privilege and the Legal Services Crisis, 79 Stan. L. Rev. Online 78 (2026)"},{"t":"AI and legal jobs","u":"lessons/ai-and-legal-jobs/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 5: Fairness, privacy and legal jobs","d":"Many people in law wonder what AI means for their jobs. Some tasks are changing quickly, and others hardly at all.","x":"Tasks AI changes most and least AI is changing tasks that repeat the same steps across many documents. These include first-pass document review, summarizing depositions and contracts, routine drafting and basic research. It is changing much less the tasks that depend on judgment, trust and people. These include advising clients, negotiating, arguing in court, managing a case strategy and handling sensitive conversations. Training new lawyers Junior lawyers and paralegals have long learned by doing routine work, such as reviewing documents. If AI does more of that work, firms will need new ways to train people. Expect this to change how new lawyers learn the job. Jobs that are growing Legal teams now need people who can choose AI tools, test them, write policies and train others. Titles include legal technologist, legal operations specialist and knowledge management lawyer. Courts and regulators also need lawyers who understand AI. How to get ready Use approved AI tools for parts of your work, and learn where they fail. Get skilled at checking AI output, including citations, facts and dates. Build skills AI finds hard, such as judgment, writing for a specific reader, negotiation and client care. Learn the rules on AI where you practice or study. The module Your professional duties covers them. Studies of AI help in real workplaces, such as customer support, writing and coding, have often found the biggest gains for less experienced workers. Economists separate tasks from jobs . AI usually takes over or helps with specific tasks, so most jobs change shape before they disappear. For law, a useful exercise is to list your tasks and mark which are routine, which need judgment and which need a human relationship. Which legal tasks is AI changing most right now? "},{"t":"Module 6: What’s next in AI","u":"modules/whats-next/","k":"Module","d":"AI agents that carry out tasks, AI that reads scans and transcribes speech, and how to keep up with the rules.","x":"AI agents carry out tasks with many steps, so your duty to supervise covers their work. AI can read scans and transcribe speech. Check recording laws and treat transcripts as drafts. Keep up by knowing your bar’s guidance, your courts’ rules and your employer’s policy."},{"t":"AI agents","u":"lessons/ai-agents/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 6: What’s next in AI","d":"AI is starting to carry out tasks for you, as well as answer questions. AI that does tasks is called an AI agent.","x":"More than chat A chatbot answers you. An AI agent takes action. You give it a goal. It breaks the goal into steps and uses tools, such as a web browser, a document system or email. It checks its work and keeps going until the task is done. Legal examples “Review these 200 supplier contracts, find every clause about ending the contract early, and put them in a table with the notice period.” Or: “Research how courts in this state have treated electronic signatures on wills, and draft a short memo with sources.” An agent can attempt each of these with little help. Agents you can use today Research agents. The “deep research” modes in ChatGPT, Gemini and Claude read dozens of sources and write a report with links. Legal research services are adding similar features. Agents that use a computer. Some assistants can operate a web browser. They click, type and fill in forms. Document agents. Some legal tools can work through a whole set of documents and fill in a review table. Stay in charge An agent that can send emails, file documents or move money needs firm limits. Your duty to supervise covers the work it does, in the same way it covers work by a junior lawyer or a vendor. Give it access only to what the task needs. Require a person to approve anything that is sent, filed or paid. Check its sources and results before you rely on them. Small error rates add up over many steps. If each step works 95 percent of the time, a ten-step task works only about 60 percent of the time. Good agents check their work, retry and ask for help. For legal work, build in review points. A person approves the research plan, checks the sources and signs off before anything is filed or sent. The lesson Agents, tools and testing shows how agents are built. What is the main difference between a chatbot and an AI agent? "},{"t":"AI that sees and hears","u":"lessons/ai-that-sees-and-hears/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 6: What’s next in AI","d":"Today’s AI can read scanned pages, photos and handwriting, and it can turn speech into text. That helps with evidence and with recording rules.","x":"Multimodal AI Multimodal means working with several kinds of information at once, such as text, pictures and sound. Most of the leading AI models are now multimodal. Reading documents AI can read scanned pages, photos of documents and some handwriting. It can turn a box of scanned records into searchable text or pull dates and amounts into a table. It can misread numbers and names, so check anything important against the original page. Turning speech into text AI can transcribe meetings, interviews and voicemails and then sum them up. Before you record anyone, check the law. In some U.S. states, everyone on a call must agree to it being recorded. Recording tools also receive confidential information, so they must be approved for that use. Not a court reporter An AI transcript is a working draft. It can mishear legal terms, names and numbers. Do not treat it as an official record of a deposition or hearing. Talking out loud Voice modes in ChatGPT, Gemini and other apps let you talk with the AI out loud and get an answer in about a second. That can help you rehearse an opening statement or practice explaining a point to a client. Keep confidential details out of these conversations unless the tool is approved for them. Pictures are cut into small squares, and each square becomes a token, so pictures and text can go through the same model. Speech-to-text models turn sound into text and can label who is speaking, though they make more mistakes with accents, cross-talk and legal terms. Reading text from scans is called optical character recognition , or OCR. Modern AI does it better than older tools, but it can still misread numbers, which matters in contracts and financial records. Before you use AI to record and transcribe a call with a client, what should you check? "},{"t":"Keeping up","u":"lessons/keeping-up/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 6: What’s next in AI","d":"AI and the rules about it change fast. You can stay informed without reading everything.","x":"How fast things change A few years ago, AI could barely write a paragraph. Now it drafts documents, holds spoken conversations and reviews contracts. New models come out every few months. Courts, bars and lawmakers are writing new rules about AI at the same time. A simple routine Know the rules that apply to you. Check your bar’s ethics guidance, the rules of the courts you appear in and your employer’s or school’s AI policy. Follow one or two trusted sources. Examples are the ABA Journal, your state bar’s news and a legal technology newsletter you trust. Use the tools. Ten minutes trying a feature on a made-up problem teaches more than an hour of reading. Come back to this site. The AI tools and AI models pages are updated every week. Where to go next If you are a lawyer, paralegal or law student, the module Your professional duties covers competence, confidentiality, courts and checking AI research. The module Legal AI tools and AI law covers choosing tools and the laws that regulate AI. For more technical detail, read the model cards and system cards that companies publish with each new model. For legal tools, ask vendors for their own test results and how they measured accuracy. Academic studies such as the Stanford RegLab study of legal research tools are a good check on vendor claims. Court standing orders and local rules are usually posted on each court’s website. What is the most reliable way to keep up with the rules on AI in legal work? "},{"t":"Module 7: How AI works inside","u":"modules/how-ai-works-inside/","k":"Module","d":"An extra module for technical readers and legal technologists. Covers tokens, training, how legal AI tools are built, agents and testing.","x":"Models read and write tokens, only see what fits in the context window, and pick each token by chance. Training gives a model knowledge up to a cutoff date. Feedback training makes it helpful, and sometimes too eager to answer. Most legal AI tools combine a model with search over real legal sources. Test them on your own kind of work."},{"t":"Tokens, context and chance","u":"lessons/tokens-and-context/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 7: How AI works inside","d":"Three basic ideas explain most of how a language model behaves, including some mistakes that matter in legal work.","x":"Tokens, not words Models do not read single letters or whole words. Text is split into tokens . A common word is usually one token. A rarer word, such as a party’s name or a Latin phrase, is split into several pieces. In English, 1,000 tokens is about 750 words. “res ipsa loquitur” → [“res”, “ ips”, “a”, “ loqu”, “itur”] (a made-up example) This explains a well-known weakness. A model sees chunks, while you see letters and digits. It can miscount letters or slip on a digit in a long number, such as a docket number or a dollar amount. Always check numbers, dates and citations against the source. The context window A model can only consider a limited amount of text at once. That text includes your instructions, the whole conversation, any documents and the answer it is writing. All of it must fit in the model’s context window , which is measured in tokens. Current models hold from about a hundred thousand to more than a million tokens, which can be several long contracts. When a conversation grows too long, apps drop or shorten the older parts. That is why a long chat can “forget” a fact from the start. Chance and temperature At each step, the model gives every possible next token a chance of being picked. Then one token is chosen. A setting called temperature controls how adventurous that choice is. Near zero, the model almost always takes the most likely token, so its answers stay much the same. Higher settings give more variety. That is why asking the same question twice can give two different answers. What this means for cost AI services for programmers, called APIs , charge by the million tokens. Tokens you send and tokens the model writes have separate prices. Sending a 300-page contract with every question gets expensive, which is one reason legal tools search for the relevant parts first.  Why might a chatbot “forget” a fact from early in a long conversation about a case? "},{"t":"How models are trained","u":"lessons/how-models-are-trained/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 7: How AI works inside","d":"A chatbot goes through several stages of training to turn raw text into a helpful assistant. Each stage explains a strength or a weakness.","x":"Stage 1: Pretraining The model learns to predict the next token across trillions of tokens of text and computer code. This takes thousands of special computer chips running for months. The result is a base model . It knows a lot, but it only continues text. It does not follow instructions yet. Stage 2: Learning from example chats Next, the base model trains on many example conversations that show helpful answers. This teaches it to act as an assistant. It learns to follow instructions, answer questions and use a steady format. Stage 3: Learning from feedback People compare pairs of answers and pick the better one. The model is then trained to give the kind of answers people picked. This is called reinforcement learning from human feedback , or RLHF. It makes answers more helpful and polite. It can also make the model eager to please, so it may give an answer when it should say “I don’t know.” Stage 4: Learning to reason Thinking models get extra training on problems with answers that can be checked, such as math and code. They are rewarded when the final answer is correct. This helps with multi-step problems. Legal answers are harder to check automatically, so this training helps less with legal judgment. Knowledge cutoff A model only knows what was in its training data. That data stops at a date called the knowledge cutoff . A new statute, rule change or appeals decision after that date has to come from a search, a legal database or documents you give it. Open and closed models Some companies publish the trained settings of their models, called weights . Anyone can then run and change those models. These are called open-weight models, such as Llama, Mistral, DeepSeek and Qwen. An organization can run them on its own computers, which keeps client data inside. Other companies only offer their models through their own apps and services.  Why might a chatbot not know about a court decision from last month? "},{"t":"Building with AI","u":"lessons/building-with-ai/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 7: How AI works inside","d":"Legal AI tools are built from a few common parts. Knowing them helps you judge a tool and talk to vendors.","x":"An API call Apps talk to models through an API , which is a set of rules that lets one program ask another program for something. Your code sends instructions and messages, and it gets a reply back. A typical request looks like this. The details differ from company to company. { “model”: “a-model-name”, “system”: “Answer only from the firm policy provided. Quote the section you relied on. If the policy does not cover it, say so.”, “messages”: [ { “role”: “user”, “content”: “Can I use my personal laptop for client work?” } ], “max_tokens”: 300 } The system prompt sets the model’s role, tone and rules for the whole conversation. Results get much more reliable when you include one or two worked examples and ask for a fixed format. Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) A model never saw your firm’s documents in training. RAG lets it answer from them anyway. Most legal research tools work this way, using a database of cases and statutes. It works in three steps. Split the documents into short chunks. Turn each chunk into an embedding , which is a list of numbers that captures its meaning. When a question comes in, turn it into an embedding too. Then find the chunks whose meaning is closest. Put those chunks into the prompt. Ask the model to answer only from them and to say which chunks it used. If the search step finds the wrong passages, the answer will be wrong too, even with real citations attached. That is one reason legal research tools still make mistakes. Prompting, RAG or fine-tuning Start with a good prompt. Add RAG when answers depend on your own documents or on law that changes. Think about fine-tuning , which means extra training on your own examples, only when you need the same style at a large scale and a prompt cannot get you there. Two safety basics Keep API keys on a server, never in a web page users can look inside. And do not trust incoming documents. Text hidden in a document, such as a file produced by the other side, can try to give the model orders. This is called prompt injection .  What does retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) do in a legal research tool? "},{"t":"Agents, tools and testing","u":"lessons/agents-tools-and-testing/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 7: How AI works inside","d":"AI agents use tools over and over until a job is done. Legal teams also need a way to check that an AI system works before they rely on it.","x":"Tools You describe tools to the model. Each description gives the tool’s name, what it does and what information it needs. Legal examples are search_case_law , open_matter_file and calculate_deadline . When a tool would help, the model replies with a request to use it. Your code runs the tool and sends the result back. Then the model carries on. The agent loop Goal Plan the next step Use a tool Read the result Repeat or finish An agent is this loop running until the goal is met. MCP , short for Model Context Protocol, is an open standard that Anthropic introduced in 2024. Many companies now support it. It lets AI apps connect to tools and information in one standard way. Safety limits Give each agent only the access it needs. Respect ethical walls, the barriers that keep information about one matter away from people working on another. Have a person approve anything that is filed, sent to a client or paid. Keep a record of every tool the agent uses, and set limits on time and spending. Testing Before you rely on an AI system, build a set of test cases, called an evaluation set . Each test case is a real task plus a description of a good answer. For legal research, that might be 50 questions where you already know the right cases. Check the answers automatically where you can, and have lawyers check a sample by hand. Run the tests again every time the tool, the model or your prompts change. Small changes often have surprising effects. Ask vendors for this Ask any legal AI vendor how they test accuracy, on what kinds of questions, and how often their citations are wrong. Then run a small test of your own on your kind of work.  When a model uses a tool, what runs the tool? "},{"t":"Module 8: Your professional duties","u":"modules/professional-duties/","k":"Module","d":"How the ethics rules on competence, confidentiality, privilege and candor to courts apply to AI, and how to check AI research.","x":"ABA Formal Opinion 512 and state bar guidance apply the existing ethics rules to AI. Check your own state’s rules. Get informed consent when required before putting client information into an AI tool, and read the tool’s terms. Check the court’s local rules and the judge’s standing orders on AI before every filing. Find, read and check every authority yourself. Never ask the AI to check itself."},{"t":"Your duties when you use AI","u":"lessons/duties-when-using-ai/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 8: Your professional duties","d":"No new rule book is needed. The ethics rules lawyers already follow apply to AI, and bars have explained how.","x":"The main guidance In July 2024, the American Bar Association issued Formal Opinion 512 on generative AI. It does not create new rules. It explains how the existing rules of professional conduct apply when lawyers use AI. Many state bars have issued their own guidance too. Florida’s Opinion 24-1 came out in January 2024. California published practical guidance in 2023 and updated it in 2026. Check your own rules The ABA’s opinions are guidance. They do not have the force of law. Your state’s rules and ethics opinions are the ones that apply to you, and they can differ. This course is general education, not legal advice. The six duties in plain language Competence Understand what the tool does well and badly, enough to use it safely. Keep learning as it changes. Check its work. Confidentiality Protect client information. Know what the tool does with what you type. Get the client’s informed consent, meaning agreement after you explain the risks, when the rules require it. Communication Tell clients about AI use when it matters to them, such as when they ask, when it affects the fee or when their information goes into a tool. Candor Never give a court false statements of law or fact. Everything you file must be checked, whoever or whatever wrote the first draft. Supervision Make sure associates, paralegals, staff and vendors use AI properly. Set policies and train people. Fees Charge reasonable fees. Bill only for time actually spent, and explain any charges for AI tools. For law students These duties start when you practice, but habits start now. Follow your school’s honor code. Practice checking AI output as if a client depended on it. Many legal employers will ask how you use AI. Opinion 512 ties each duty to the ABA Model Rules. Competence is Rule 1.1. Confidentiality is Rules 1.6, 1.9(c) and 1.18(b). Communication is Rule 1.4. Candor and meritorious claims are Rules 3.1, 3.3 and 8.4(c). Supervision is Rules 5.1 and 5.3, and fees are Rule 1.5. The Model Rules are a template. Each state adopts its own version, sometimes with changes, and state ethics opinions can differ from the ABA’s. According to ABA Formal Opinion 512, what level of understanding of AI do lawyers need? ABA Comm. on Ethics & Pro. Resp., Formal Op. 512, Generative Artificial Intelligence Tools (July 29, 2024) Fla. Bar Ethics Op. 24-1 (Jan. 19, 2024) State Bar of Cal., Ethics & Technology Resources (generative AI practical guidance)"},{"t":"Confidentiality and privilege","u":"lessons/confidentiality-and-privilege/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 8: Your professional duties","d":"Client information is the thing lawyers must protect most. AI tools raise new questions about where that information goes.","x":"Two different protections Confidentiality is a lawyer’s ethical duty to protect all information about a client’s matter. Attorney-client privilege is a rule of evidence. It keeps private communications between a client and lawyer, made to get legal advice, out of court. Sharing privileged material with outsiders can waive the privilege, which means losing it. Where AI creates risk The tool keeps or learns from what you type. Then client information could show up in the company’s systems or, in rare cases, in other users’ answers. People at the company can read it. Some companies review chats for safety. Someone else can demand it. Stored chats can be requested in a lawsuit or by the government. Clients use AI on their own. In United States v. Heppner (2026), a federal judge held that a defendant’s own chats with an AI tool were not privileged. The lesson Privacy and your data explains the case. What the ABA says ABA Formal Opinion 512 says lawyers should understand how a tool uses the information they put in. Before putting confidential information into a tool that may learn from it or share it, lawyers generally need the client’s informed consent . General standard wording in an engagement letter is not enough. Florida’s Opinion 24-1 also says lawyers should get informed consent before disclosing confidential information to a third-party AI tool. A practical checklist Use only tools your employer has approved for client data. Read the terms about training, data storage and access. Remove names and identifying details when you can. Get informed consent when the rules require it. Warn clients that their own AI chats about the case may not be private. When you review a vendor, ask whether inputs and outputs are used for training, how long they are kept, where they are stored, who at the vendor can access them, what security certifications the vendor holds, which other companies process the data, and how the vendor responds to subpoenas and breaches. Get the answers written into the contract. Also check whether the tool keeps data from different matters separate, so ethical walls hold. Under ABA Formal Opinion 512, what is usually needed before putting confidential client information into an AI tool that may learn from it? ABA Comm. on Ethics & Pro. Resp., Formal Op. 512, Generative Artificial Intelligence Tools (July 29, 2024) Fla. Bar Ethics Op. 24-1 (Jan. 19, 2024) United States v. Heppner, No. 25-cr-503 (JSR), ECF No. 27 (S.D.N.Y. Feb. 17, 2026) (scanned opinion)"},{"t":"Courts, candor and AI rules","u":"lessons/courts-and-ai/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 8: Your professional duties","d":"Courts expect the same accuracy whether a person or a machine wrote the first draft. Some courts and judges have added their own AI rules.","x":"The duty that does not change When you sign a court filing, you vouch for it. In federal court, Rule 11 says your signature means the legal arguments are supported by the law after a reasonable inquiry. The ethics rules also forbid false statements of law or fact to a court. It makes no difference whether you, a colleague or an AI wrote the first draft. This is why the lawyers in Mata v. Avianca were sanctioned. The fake cases were bad enough. They also failed to come clean when the court asked about them. Judges’ and courts’ own AI rules In May 2023, Judge Brantley Starr of the U.S. District Court for the Northern District of Texas became one of the first judges to issue an order on generative AI. Lawyers had to file a certificate saying either that no part of their filing was drafted by generative AI, or that a person checked any AI-drafted language against print reporters or traditional legal databases. The court later made a rule for every case. Its Local Civil Rule 7.2(f) says a brief prepared using generative AI must say so on its first page, under the heading “Use of Generative Artificial Intelligence.” The judge can ask which parts AI prepared. Filing a brief without that statement counts as certifying that no part of it was prepared with generative AI. Judge Starr no longer requires his separate certificate. Many other judges and courts have issued their own rules, and they differ. Some ask you to disclose AI use, some ask you to certify that you checked it, and a few limit it. Before every filing Check the court’s local rules (its own rules for every case) and the assigned judge’s standing orders (that judge’s own rules) for anything about AI. Follow them exactly. If you used AI, keep a note of what you used it for and how you checked the result. If you find a mistake after filing Correct it quickly. The duty of candor requires lawyers to fix false statements they have made to a court. In Mata v. Avianca , the court stressed that the lawyers made things worse by standing by the fake cases after the court questioned them. In federal court, Rule 11 of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure says that by signing a filing, a lawyer certifies that its legal contentions are warranted by existing law or a nonfrivolous argument, after a reasonable inquiry. Model Rule 3.3 forbids knowingly making false statements of law or fact to a tribunal and requires correcting them. Many state courts have their own versions of both rules. Under the Northern District of Texas’s Local Civil Rule 7.2(f), what must a brief prepared using generative AI include? N.D. Tex. Loc. Civ. R. 7.2(f) (Local Civil Rules, eff. Sept. 1, 2026) U.S. Dist. Ct. for the N.D. Tex., Judge Brantley Starr, Judge-Specific Requirements Mata v. Avianca, Inc., 678 F. Supp. 3d 443 (S.D.N.Y. 2023) (opinion and order on sanctions) ABA Comm. on Ethics & Pro. Resp., Formal Op. 512, Generative Artificial Intelligence Tools (July 29, 2024)"},{"t":"Checking AI legal research","u":"lessons/checking-ai-legal-research/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 8: Your professional duties","d":"AI can speed up research, but every case, quote and statute it gives you must be checked. A short routine makes the checking reliable.","x":"Why a routine matters AI mistakes in legal research look exactly like real research. A made-up case has a plausible name, a real-looking citation and a convincing summary. A real case can be cited for something it never said. The Stanford study of legal research tools found both kinds of errors. Only a steady checking routine catches them. A checking routine Find it. Pull every case, statute and rule from a trusted legal database or an official court or government website. If you cannot find it, do not use it. Read it. Read the full passage you rely on. A summary is not enough. Make sure it supports your point. Check it is still good law. Run it through a citator such as Shepard’s or KeyCite. Check that statutes and rules are the current versions. Check the details. Compare every quote, page number, court and date with the original. Check where it applies. Make sure your court must follow the authority, or at least is likely to find it persuasive. Do not ask the AI to check itself Asking “Is this case real?” proves nothing. The AI can confirm a fake case as confidently as it invented it. Use sources outside the AI. Good uses of AI in research AI is useful for getting oriented in an unfamiliar area, suggesting search terms, listing issues to consider and summing up a case you have already found and read. Treat its suggestions as leads to follow, never as authority. Supervising research by others If a junior lawyer, paralegal or student researcher used AI, ask how they checked the results. Your signature on the filing makes the research yours. Errors in AI legal research fall into a few types. The authority may not exist. It may exist but say something different. It may be real but no longer good law. It may be from the wrong jurisdiction or court level. A quote or page number may be wrong. A good review process checks for each type. For a high-stakes filing, a second person can check citations independently. Keep a short record of the checks, which helps if a court or client asks how AI was used. An AI tool gives you a case with a real-looking citation. What is the first thing to do? Varun Magesh et al., Hallucination-Free? Assessing the Reliability of Leading AI Legal Research Tools, 22 J. Empirical Legal Stud. 216 (2025) Park v. Kim, 91 F.4th 610 (2d Cir. 2024) Mata v. Avianca, Inc., 678 F. Supp. 3d 443 (S.D.N.Y. 2023) (opinion and order on sanctions)"},{"t":"Module 9: Legal AI tools and AI law","u":"modules/legal-tools-and-law/","k":"Module","d":"The main kinds of legal AI tools, how to choose one, and the laws that regulate AI.","x":"Legal AI tools ground answers in real sources and protect data better, but they still make mistakes. Choose a tool for a specific task, get data promises in the contract and test it on your own work. The EU AI Act sorts AI by risk. U.S. rules come from existing laws, states, cities, courts and bars."},{"t":"Legal AI tools","u":"lessons/legal-ai-tools/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 9: Legal AI tools and AI law","d":"Many AI tools are built for legal work. They fall into a few groups, and each has its own strengths and risks.","x":"The main kinds of legal AI tools Research AI features inside legal research services, such as Lexis+ with Protégé (called Lexis+ AI until February 2026) and Westlaw’s AI tools. You ask a question in plain English and get an answer with links to cases and statutes. Drafting and contracts Tools that draft clauses, compare contract versions or flag risky terms. Some work inside Microsoft Word. Document review E-discovery platforms that rank and sort documents in a lawsuit, and newer tools that sum up and answer questions about large sets of documents. Legal assistants General-purpose AI platforms built for law firms and legal departments, which combine research, drafting and document review. Practice management AI features in the software firms use for time tracking, billing, intake and client communication. The AI tools page lists some well-known tools in each group, with links. It is a starting point, not a recommendation. What they do better than general chatbots Legal tools are usually connected to real, current legal sources. They often come with contracts that protect client data, and they are built around legal tasks. For client work, this makes them a better fit than a free consumer chatbot. What they still get wrong They can still give wrong answers. The Stanford study found that leading legal research tools gave false or misleading answers between 17 and 33 percent of the time, including answers that cited real cases for points those cases do not support. A link to a real case is a good start, not proof. Everything in the lesson Checking AI legal research still applies. Most legal AI products combine a general language model, licensed from a large AI company or run privately, with the vendor’s own data and workflows. Examples are a database of cases, a library of clauses, or a document review platform. The vendor’s value is often in the data, the checking steps and the security, as much as in the model. That is why it is worth asking which model a product uses and what the vendor adds. How do legal research AI tools usually differ from general chatbots? Varun Magesh et al., Hallucination-Free? Assessing the Reliability of Leading AI Legal Research Tools, 22 J. Empirical Legal Stud. 216 (2025)"},{"t":"Choosing a legal AI tool","u":"lessons/choosing-a-legal-ai-tool/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 9: Legal AI tools and AI law","d":"Before a firm, court or legal department adopts an AI tool, a few careful questions can prevent big problems.","x":"Start with the problem Pick one task that takes a lot of time and has a clear right answer, such as first-pass contract review or summing up deposition transcripts. Buying a tool because it is popular, without a specific task in mind, rarely works. Questions to ask the vendor Data. Do you train on our data? How long do you keep it? Where is it stored, and who can see it? Security. What security checks and certifications do you have? What happens if there is a data breach? Accuracy. How do you test accuracy? On what kinds of tasks? How often are citations wrong? Sources. Where does the legal content come from, and how current is it? Control. Can we limit who sees which matters? Can we export or delete our data? Cost. How is it priced, and how will we explain any costs to clients? Get it in writing Put promises about data use and security in the contract. Involve whoever handles your IT security and your ethics or risk questions. Run a small trial Test the tool with a small group, using made-up documents or documents you are allowed to use. Compare its results with work by experienced people. Count the time spent checking its output as well as the time it saves. Decide ahead of time what would make you stop. After you adopt it Write a short policy on what the tool may and may not be used for. Train people on how to check its work. Review the policy as the tool and the rules change. A simple test plan has four parts. First, pick 30 to 50 tasks that look like your real work, such as research questions or contracts to review, where experienced lawyers already know the right answer. Second, run them through the tool and record every answer. Third, have lawyers grade the answers for accuracy and for errors that would cause harm. Fourth, repeat the test after major updates. Include the time spent checking when you measure time saved. What is the best way to judge whether a legal AI tool is accurate enough for your work? "},{"t":"Laws that regulate AI","u":"lessons/laws-that-regulate-ai/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 9: Legal AI tools and AI law","d":"Lawmakers are writing rules for AI itself. Clients will ask about them, and they change often.","x":"The European Union’s AI Act The EU AI Act is the most complete AI law so far. It sorts AI systems by risk. Banned A short list of uses seen as too harmful, such as social scoring that leads to unfair treatment of people. High risk Uses that affect people’s lives, such as hiring, credit, education and access to public services. These carry strict duties for testing, records, human oversight and transparency. Lower risk Lighter duties, such as telling people they are talking to a chatbot or that content was made by AI. The law applies in stages. In June 2026, the EU gave final approval to changes that moved the main high-risk deadlines to December 2, 2027, and August 2, 2028. The law can also apply to companies outside the EU that offer AI in the EU. Always check the current dates. The United States The U.S. has no single law that covers all AI. Rules come from several places. Existing laws. Laws against discrimination, unfair business practices and privacy violations already apply when AI is used. States. Colorado passed an AI law in 2024 about high-risk AI in big decisions about people. A 2025 law pushed its start date back to June 30, 2026. Check the Colorado legislature’s website for any later changes. Other states have passed narrower laws. Cities. New York City’s Local Law 144 requires bias audits for AI hiring tools. Courts and bars. Court orders and ethics opinions govern how lawyers use AI, as earlier lessons showed. Check current law AI law is changing quickly. Treat anything in this lesson as a starting point, and check current official sources before advising a client. The EU AI Act entered into force in August 2024 and applies in stages. Bans on certain practices came first, then rules for general-purpose AI models, then rules for high-risk systems. In June 2026, the Council of the EU gave final approval to a package of changes called the digital omnibus on AI. It moves the high-risk deadlines to December 2, 2027, for stand-alone systems and August 2, 2028, for AI built into regulated products. Check the version in force on EUR-Lex before advising anyone. In April 2023, several U.S. federal agencies, including the FTC and the EEOC, issued a joint statement that existing laws on consumer protection and discrimination apply to AI. Agency priorities have shifted since, so check current guidance. How does the EU AI Act sort AI systems? Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (Artificial Intelligence Act), OJ L, 12.7.2024 Council of the EU, Artificial Intelligence: Council Gives Final Green Light to Simplify and Streamline Rules (June 2026), republished by the EEAS Colo. S.B. 24-205 (2024), Consumer Protections for Artificial Intelligence Colo. S.B. 25B-004 (2025) (moving S.B. 24-205’s start date to June 30, 2026) N.Y.C. Local Law 144 of 2021, Automated Employment Decision Tools (Dep’t of Consumer & Worker Prot.)"},{"t":"Module 10: AI by practice area","u":"modules/practice-areas/","k":"Module","d":"How AI shows up in litigation and discovery, in contracts and deals, and on in-house legal teams.","x":"Litigators can use AI for review, summaries and drafts, but must check every citation and quote and follow court rules on AI. Transactional lawyers can speed up contract review, but should check a sample by hand and decide the hard calls themselves. In-house teams use AI and also set the company’s AI rules, including for high-risk uses such as hiring."},{"t":"AI in litigation and discovery","u":"lessons/ai-in-litigation/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 10: AI by practice area","d":"Litigators, the lawyers who handle lawsuits, use AI to review documents, sum up testimony and draft. Each use comes with its own risks and rules.","x":"Where AI helps litigators Document review. Tools rank or sort documents by how likely they are to be relevant or privileged. Courts have accepted this kind of review since Da Silva Moore in 2012. Summaries. Deposition transcripts, hearing transcripts and large email sets can be summed up quickly. Timelines and chronologies. Pulling dates and events from many documents into one table. Drafting. First drafts of routine motions, discovery requests and letters to opposing counsel. The risks that matter most Invented or misread authority. The sanctions in Mata v. Avianca came from a brief. Every citation in a filing must be found and read. Misstated testimony. A summary can put words in a witness’s mouth or give the wrong page and line. Missed documents. A review tool can miss relevant or privileged documents. Check its work with sampling, and agree on the method with the other side where needed. Confidential and protected material. A protective order is a court order that limits how documents shared in a lawsuit may be used. Documents produced under one may only be used and shared as the order allows. Check before loading them into any AI tool. Court rules on AI Some courts now require a disclosure when a brief was prepared using generative AI. One example is the Northern District of Texas’s Local Civil Rule 7.2(f). Check the local rules and the judge’s standing orders before every filing. Discovery about AI AI also shows up as evidence. Parties may ask for the prompts and outputs someone created, or for information about how a company’s AI tool made a decision. Think early about where your client’s AI chats are stored and whether they need to be preserved. Technology-assisted review is usually checked with sampling. A team reviews a random sample of the documents the tool marked as not relevant to estimate how many relevant documents were missed, and reports measures such as recall. Generative review tools that read each document and explain their call can be checked the same way. Parties often agree on the review method, the validation steps and how results will be reported in an ESI protocol, the plan for handling electronically stored information. A colleague used AI to sum up a 300-page deposition. What should happen before the summary is used in a brief? Da Silva Moore v. Publicis Groupe, 287 F.R.D. 182 (S.D.N.Y. 2012) Mata v. Avianca, Inc., 678 F. Supp. 3d 443 (S.D.N.Y. 2023) (opinion and order on sanctions) N.D. Tex. Loc. Civ. R. 7.2(f) (Local Civil Rules, eff. Sept. 1, 2026) Maura R. Grossman & Gordon V. Cormack, Technology-Assisted Review in E-Discovery Can Be More Effective and More Efficient Than Exhaustive Manual Review, 17 Rich. J.L. & Tech. 11 (2011)"},{"t":"AI in contracts and deals","u":"lessons/ai-in-contracts-and-deals/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 10: AI by practice area","d":"Transactional lawyers, who handle contracts and deals, use AI to review contracts, compare versions and check drafts against their preferred terms. Speed is the benefit, and missed terms are the risk.","x":"Where AI helps in transactional work First-pass review. Flag clauses across many contracts, such as what happens if a company is sold (change of control), transfers to someone else (assignment), exclusivity, automatic renewal and limits on liability. Playbook checks. A playbook lists your organization’s preferred terms and the fallback terms it will accept. Compare the other side’s draft against it. Comparing versions. Explain in plain English what changed between two drafts. Due diligence. Before a deal, buyers review the other company’s documents in a data room, a secure online folder. AI can sum up the documents and build a table of key terms. Plain-language summaries of key terms for a business client. The risks that matter most Missed terms. Unusual wording, side letters and amendments are easy to miss. Check a sample by hand, including documents the tool cleared. Wrong law. Whether a clause is enforceable depends on the governing law and the facts. AI often answers as if one rule applies everywhere. Confidentiality. Deal documents are often covered by confidentiality agreements that limit who may see them. Check those terms and use only approved tools. Numbers. Dates, amounts and notice periods can be misread, especially from scans. Check them against the original. Ask for the exact clause Ask the tool to quote the exact clause and say where it appears, such as the section number. A quote you can find in seconds is far easier to check than a paraphrase. Your judgment still decides AI can tell you a clause exists. It cannot tell you whether the client should accept it. That depends on the deal, the client’s goals and the negotiating position, which is the lawyer’s job. Contract review tools usually combine a language model with a clause library or playbook, which lists the firm’s preferred positions, fallback positions and terms to reject. Results are more reliable when the tool is asked to quote the exact clause and give its location, so a reviewer can check it quickly. Scanned contracts depend on text recognition, which can misread numbers. Large reviews are often checked by measuring how many known clauses the tool found in a hand-reviewed sample. An AI tool reviewed 200 supplier contracts and listed every change-of-control clause. What is the right next step? "},{"t":"AI for in-house teams and legal operations","u":"lessons/ai-in-house/","k":"Lesson","m":"Module 10: AI by practice area","d":"In-house lawyers, who work for one company, and legal operations teams often decide which AI tools a company uses and write the rules for using them.","x":"Two jobs at once In-house teams use AI for their own work, such as reviewing contracts and answering routine questions from the business. They also advise the whole company on how it uses AI. The second job often matters more. Using AI in the legal team Contract intake and review for routine agreements, using the company’s playbook. Answering common questions from employees, using the company’s own policies as the source. Tracking matters and outside counsel spend, including reviewing invoices. Advising the business on AI Approving tools. Review what data a tool touches, where it goes and what the contract promises. The vendor questions resource can help. Spotting high-risk uses. AI that helps decide who gets hired, approved for credit or offered services can trigger specific laws, such as New York City’s bias-audit law for hiring tools and the EU AI Act’s rules for high-risk uses. Writing the rules. A short company AI policy, training and a simple way for employees to ask before using a new tool. Contracts with customers. Customers increasingly ask how the company uses AI with their data. Contract terms and privacy notices need to match what happens in practice. Legal operations Legal operations teams manage the legal department’s tools, budgets, data and processes. They often lead AI tool trials, measure time saved and train lawyers. The Choosing a legal AI tool lesson covers how to run a fair trial. Many companies set up an AI review process that sorts proposed uses by risk. Low-risk uses, such as drafting internal emails, get a quick approval. High-risk uses, such as decisions about hiring, credit or customers’ rights, get a fuller review of data, testing, human oversight and notices. Frameworks such as the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and the EU AI Act’s risk categories are often used as a starting structure. The marketing team wants to use a new AI tool to screen job applicants. Why should the legal team review it first? N.Y.C. Local Law 144 of 2021, Automated Employment Decision Tools (Dep’t of Consumer & Worker Prot.) Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (Artificial Intelligence Act), OJ L, 12.7.2024"},{"t":"AI use checklist","u":"resources/ai-use-checklist/","k":"Resource","d":"A one-page check to run before, during and after you use AI on legal work.","x":"Based on the duties described in ABA Formal Opinion 512 and state bar guidance. Your own state’s rules and your employer’s policy come first. Before you start The tool is approved by my employer or school for this kind of work. I know whether the tool keeps, trains on or shares what I type. I will not enter client names, privileged material or other confidential information unless the tool is approved for it. If the rules require it, the client has given informed consent to using this tool with their information. I have checked the court’s local rules and the judge’s standing orders on AI, if this is for a filing. For coursework, I have checked my school’s and professor’s AI rules. While you work I use made-up names and only the facts the task needs. My prompt names my role, the jurisdiction, the task and the format I want. I ask the tool to flag what it is unsure about. I keep a short note of what I used AI for on this matter. Before it leaves your desk I found and read every case, statute and rule cited, from a trusted source. Each authority is still good law, checked with a citator. Every quote, page number, date and dollar amount matches the original. The facts are right for this client and this matter. I rewrote the draft in my own words and I stand behind every sentence. I made any AI disclosure the court or client requires. My bill reflects time I actually spent, and any AI costs have been explained to the client. If you supervise others The people I supervise know our AI policy and which tools are approved. I ask how they checked any AI-assisted work before I rely on it or sign it."},{"t":"Citation checking worksheet","u":"resources/citation-worksheet/","k":"Resource","d":"A printable worksheet for checking every authority in a document, whether or not AI helped write it.","x":"Use one row for each authority. Courts have sanctioned lawyers for citing cases that did not exist. Asking an AI tool whether a case is real proves nothing. The five checks Find it in a trusted legal database or on an official court or government website. Read it. Read the passage you rely on. A summary is not enough. Good law. Run it through a citator. Confirm statutes and rules are current. Details. Match every quote, pin cite, court and date to the original. Fit. Confirm it is binding or persuasive in this court. Worksheet Document: ______________________________ Matter: ______________________ Checked by: ______________ Date: __________ The worksheet table is made for printing or a larger screen. Use Print or save as a PDF above to get a copy you can fill in. On a phone, the five checks above work as a checklist. # Authority as cited Found in Read Good law Quotes and pin cites match Binding or persuasive here Notes 1 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 2 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 3 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 4 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 5 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 6 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 7 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ 8 ☐ ☐ ☐ ☐ If you cannot find an authority, do not use it. If you find a mistake after filing, correct it promptly."},{"t":"Questions to ask an AI vendor","u":"resources/vendor-questions/","k":"Resource","d":"Questions to ask before a firm, court, school or legal department adopts an AI tool.","x":"Get the answers written into the contract. A demo or a sales page is not enough. Involve whoever handles IT security and ethics or risk questions. Your data Do you train your models, or anyone else’s, on what we enter or upload? How long do you keep our inputs and outputs? Can we choose zero retention? Where is our data stored, and in which countries? Which of your staff and which other companies can see our data, and why? How do you respond to a subpoena or government request for our data? Security Which independent security audits or certifications do you hold? How will you tell us about a data breach, and how quickly? Can we sign in with our own accounts and turn on two-step sign-in? Accuracy How do you measure accuracy, on what kinds of tasks, and can we see the results? How often are citations wrong or unsupported in your testing? Which AI model does the product use, and how will we know when it changes? Can we run a trial on our own test tasks before we buy? Legal sources Where does the legal content come from, and how current is it? Does every answer link to the source it relied on? Control Can we limit who sees which matters, so ethical walls hold? Is there a record of who used the tool, when and on what? Can we export and delete all of our data when we leave? Cost How is it priced, and what costs extra? How will we explain any AI charges to clients?"},{"t":"Sample AI policy for a legal team","u":"resources/sample-ai-policy/","k":"Resource","d":"A short starting point for a law firm or legal department policy on generative AI. Adapt it to your own rules.","x":"Read this first This is a sample to adapt, not legal advice. Ethics rules and court rules differ by state and court. Review it with whoever handles ethics and risk for your organization. Replace everything in [brackets]. 1. Purpose This policy explains how people at [organization] may use generative AI tools in their work. It applies to lawyers, paralegals, staff, contractors and students working with us. 2. Approved tools Use only the tools listed in [location of the approved list] for work that involves client or matter information. [Name or team] approves tools after reviewing how they store, use and protect data. Public chatbots on personal accounts may be used only with made-up or public information. 3. Client information Do not enter confidential client information into any tool that is not approved for it. Before entering client information into a tool that may keep, learn from or share it, get the client’s informed consent where the rules require it. Remove names and identifying details when the task does not need them. 4. Checking AI output The person who uses AI is responsible for the result. Check every fact, date, number, quote and legal authority against a trusted source before relying on it or sending it. Find and read every case, statute and rule cited, and confirm it is still good law. 5. Court filings Before filing, check the court’s local rules and the judge’s standing orders on AI and follow them, including any disclosure. A lawyer who signs a filing must have personally confirmed every authority in it. 6. Supervision and training Supervising lawyers must make sure the people they supervise follow this policy. Everyone who uses approved tools completes [training] before first use and when the tools or this policy change. 7. Billing Bill only for time actually spent. Do not bill clients for time spent learning a general AI tool. Explain any charges for AI tools to clients in advance, in writing. 8. Records Keep a short note in the matter file when AI was used for substantive work, including the tool and how the output was checked. 9. Problems and questions Report any mistake, data exposure or concern to [contact] right away. Ask [contact] if you are unsure whether a use is allowed. 10. Review [Name or team] reviews this policy at least every [six] months and when the law or ethics guidance changes. Last reviewed: [date]."},{"t":"Bar guidance on AI","u":"resources/bar-guidance/","k":"Resource","d":"Ethics opinions and guidance on lawyers’ use of generative AI from the ABA and several state bars, with links to the originals.","x":"This list is a starting point, not a complete survey. Many other states have issued guidance, and opinions are updated. Check your own bar’s website for the current version. American Bar Association Formal Opinion 512, Generative Artificial Intelligence Tools (July 29, 2024). The national baseline. Covers competence, confidentiality, communication, candor, supervision and fees. Read the opinion (opens in a new tab) California State Bar of California practical guidance on generative AI, first approved in November 2023 and updated in 2026. Ethics and Technology Resources (opens in a new tab) District of Columbia D.C. Bar Ethics Opinion 388, Attorneys’ Use of Generative Artificial Intelligence in Client Matters (2024). Stresses that AI output does not replace checking facts and citations. Read the opinion (opens in a new tab) Florida Florida Bar Ethics Opinion 24-1 (January 19, 2024). Recommends informed client consent before disclosing confidential information to a third-party AI tool, and cautions against charging for time spent learning AI. Read the opinion (opens in a new tab) Kentucky Kentucky Bar Association Ethics Opinion KBA E-457 (2024). Listed on the KBA’s ethics opinions page under 2024. KBA ethics opinions (opens in a new tab) North Carolina 2024 Formal Ethics Opinion 1, Use of Artificial Intelligence in a Law Practice (adopted November 1, 2024). Lawyers may use AI if they do so competently, protect confidentiality and supervise the work. Read the opinion (opens in a new tab) Texas Professional Ethics Committee Opinion 705 (February 2025). Among other points, lawyers should not bill hourly for time saved by using AI. Read the opinion (opens in a new tab) Other places to check Bars and courts in other states, including New York, New Jersey and Pennsylvania, have also issued reports, guidelines or opinions on AI. Search your state bar’s ethics opinions and your courts’ websites for the current versions. For court filings, also check local rules and each judge’s standing orders."},{"t":"ABA Formal Opinion 512","u":"glossary/#term-aba-formal-opinion-512","k":"Glossary","d":"The American Bar Association’s July 2024 ethics opinion on lawyers’ use of generative AI. It covers competence, confidentiality, communication, candor, supervision and fees.","x":""},{"t":"AGI (artificial general intelligence)","u":"glossary/#term-agi-artificial-general-intelligence","k":"Glossary","d":"An AI that could do any thinking task as well as a person. It does not exist yet, and experts disagree about whether or when it will.","x":""},{"t":"AI agent","u":"glossary/#term-ai-agent","k":"Glossary","d":"An AI that takes actions to reach a goal. It might browse the web, fill in forms or write code, as well as reply with text.","x":""},{"t":"AI bias","u":"glossary/#term-ai-bias","k":"Glossary","d":"When an AI gives unfair results for some groups of people. It usually happens because the training data was one-sided.","x":""},{"t":"AI model","u":"glossary/#term-ai-model","k":"Glossary","d":"The trained AI system inside an app that does the work. ChatGPT is an app. The GPT models are the models inside it.","x":""},{"t":"API","u":"glossary/#term-api","k":"Glossary","d":"A set of rules that lets one program ask another program for something. Apps send requests to an AI model’s API and get replies back.","x":""},{"t":"Artificial intelligence (AI)","u":"glossary/#term-artificial-intelligence-ai","k":"Glossary","d":"Computer systems that do jobs that usually need human thinking, like understanding speech, making choices or writing text.","x":""},{"t":"Attorney-client privilege","u":"glossary/#term-attorney-client-privilege","k":"Glossary","d":"A rule of evidence that keeps private communications between a client and lawyer, made to get legal advice, out of court. Sharing them with outsiders can waive it.","x":""},{"t":"Bias audit","u":"glossary/#term-bias-audit","k":"Glossary","d":"A test that compares an AI tool’s results across groups of people, such as by sex and race, to find unfair differences.","x":""},{"t":"Candor to the court","u":"glossary/#term-candor-to-the-court","k":"Glossary","d":"A lawyer’s duty not to make false statements of law or fact to a court, and to correct them if made.","x":""},{"t":"Chatbot","u":"glossary/#term-chatbot","k":"Glossary","d":"An app you can talk to in everyday language, such as ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini or Copilot.","x":""},{"t":"Citator","u":"glossary/#term-citator","k":"Glossary","d":"A service, such as Shepard’s or KeyCite, that shows whether a case or statute is still good law and which later cases cite it.","x":""},{"t":"Confidentiality","u":"glossary/#term-confidentiality","k":"Glossary","d":"A lawyer’s ethical duty to protect all information about a client’s matter. That covers far more than privileged communications.","x":""},{"t":"Content credentials","u":"glossary/#term-content-credentials","k":"Glossary","d":"A label attached to a file that records whether and how AI was used to make or change it. Some cameras, apps and websites show it.","x":""},{"t":"Context window","u":"glossary/#term-context-window","k":"Glossary","d":"How much text an AI can consider at once, including your conversation and any documents. A bigger window can handle longer material.","x":""},{"t":"Deep learning","u":"glossary/#term-deep-learning","k":"Glossary","d":"A strong kind of machine learning. It uses neural networks with many layers to find complicated patterns.","x":""},{"t":"Deepfake","u":"glossary/#term-deepfake","k":"Glossary","d":"A realistic fake photo, video or voice recording of a real person, made with AI. Deepfakes raise new questions about evidence.","x":""},{"t":"Diffusion model","u":"glossary/#term-diffusion-model","k":"Glossary","d":"The kind of AI behind most picture and video tools. It starts with random static and cleans it up into a picture that matches your description.","x":""},{"t":"E-discovery","u":"glossary/#term-e-discovery","k":"Glossary","d":"Finding, collecting and reviewing electronic information, such as emails and files, for a lawsuit.","x":""},{"t":"Embedding","u":"glossary/#term-embedding","k":"Glossary","d":"A list of numbers that stands for the meaning of a piece of text. It lets a computer quickly find texts with similar meanings.","x":""},{"t":"Ethical wall","u":"glossary/#term-ethical-wall","k":"Glossary","d":"Barriers inside a firm that keep people working on one matter from seeing information about another, to avoid conflicts.","x":""},{"t":"EU AI Act","u":"glossary/#term-eu-ai-act","k":"Glossary","d":"The European Union’s law on AI. It sorts AI systems by risk, with the strictest duties for high-risk uses such as hiring and credit.","x":""},{"t":"Evaluation set","u":"glossary/#term-evaluation-set","k":"Glossary","d":"A set of test examples used to measure how well an AI system works. Teams run it again after every change. Also called evals.","x":""},{"t":"Fine-tuning","u":"glossary/#term-fine-tuning","k":"Glossary","d":"Extra training for a model that already exists, using special examples. It makes the model better at one task or style.","x":""},{"t":"Generative AI","u":"glossary/#term-generative-ai","k":"Glossary","d":"AI that makes new things, such as text, pictures, sound, video or code. Older AI mostly sorted or checked things that already existed.","x":""},{"t":"Hallucination","u":"glossary/#term-hallucination","k":"Glossary","d":"When an AI states something false as if it were true, such as a made-up case, a wrong citation or an invented quote.","x":""},{"t":"Informed consent","u":"glossary/#term-informed-consent","k":"Glossary","d":"A client’s agreement after the lawyer has explained the risks and the other options. Some AI uses need it.","x":""},{"t":"Knowledge cutoff","u":"glossary/#term-knowledge-cutoff","k":"Glossary","d":"The date a model’s training data ends. The model knows nothing after that date unless it searches the web or you give it documents.","x":""},{"t":"Large language model (LLM)","u":"glossary/#term-large-language-model-llm","k":"Glossary","d":"An AI trained on a huge amount of text. It can write, sum up, translate and hold conversations. Chatbots run on these models.","x":""},{"t":"Machine learning","u":"glossary/#term-machine-learning","k":"Glossary","d":"A way of building AI where the computer learns from examples. Nobody writes all its rules by hand. More good examples usually make it better.","x":""},{"t":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","u":"glossary/#term-mcp-model-context-protocol","k":"Glossary","d":"An open standard for connecting AI apps to tools and information in one standard way.","x":""},{"t":"Multimodal AI","u":"glossary/#term-multimodal-ai","k":"Glossary","d":"AI that works with several kinds of information, such as text, pictures, sound and video, often at the same time.","x":""},{"t":"Neural network","u":"glossary/#term-neural-network","k":"Glossary","d":"Layers of simple math, loosely inspired by the brain. Together they turn an input, like a photo, into an output, like the word “cat.”","x":""},{"t":"Open model","u":"glossary/#term-open-model","k":"Glossary","d":"A model whose trained settings are published, so anyone can download, run and change it. Examples are Meta’s Llama and models from Mistral and DeepSeek. Also called an open-weight model.","x":""},{"t":"Parameters","u":"glossary/#term-parameters","k":"Glossary","d":"The numbers inside a model that store what it learned. Training adjusts them. Large models have billions of them.","x":""},{"t":"Prompt","u":"glossary/#term-prompt","k":"Glossary","d":"The message or instructions you give an AI. Clear, detailed prompts with some background get better results.","x":""},{"t":"Prompt injection","u":"glossary/#term-prompt-injection","k":"Glossary","d":"Hidden or harmful instructions inside a document, email or web page. They try to make an AI do something it shouldn’t.","x":""},{"t":"Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)","u":"glossary/#term-retrieval-augmented-generation-rag","k":"Glossary","d":"A method where the AI first looks up useful documents and then writes its answer from them. It cuts down on made-up facts.","x":""},{"t":"RLHF (reinforcement learning from human feedback)","u":"glossary/#term-rlhf-reinforcement-learning-from-human-feedback","k":"Glossary","d":"Training a model to give the kinds of answers that people rated as better.","x":""},{"t":"Rule 11","u":"glossary/#term-rule-11","k":"Glossary","d":"The federal court rule that makes a lawyer’s signature on a filing a promise that its legal arguments are supported by the law after a reasonable inquiry.","x":""},{"t":"Standing order","u":"glossary/#term-standing-order","k":"Glossary","d":"A judge’s own rules for every case before that judge. Some cover how lawyers may use AI.","x":""},{"t":"System prompt","u":"glossary/#term-system-prompt","k":"Glossary","d":"Instructions given to a model before the conversation starts. They set its role, its tone and its rules.","x":""},{"t":"Technology-assisted review (TAR)","u":"glossary/#term-technology-assisted-review-tar","k":"Glossary","d":"Using machine learning to sort documents in a lawsuit by how likely they are to be relevant. Also called predictive coding.","x":""},{"t":"Temperature","u":"glossary/#term-temperature","k":"Glossary","d":"A setting that controls how predictable an AI’s answers are. A low setting gives steady answers. A high setting gives more varied and surprising ones.","x":""},{"t":"Thinking model","u":"glossary/#term-thinking-model","k":"Glossary","d":"A model, or a setting in a chatbot, that works through a problem step by step before it answers. It is slower and better at hard tasks. Also called a reasoning model.","x":""},{"t":"Token","u":"glossary/#term-token","k":"Glossary","d":"A chunk of text, about a word or part of a word. Language models read and write tokens. Usage limits are often counted in tokens.","x":""},{"t":"Tool use","u":"glossary/#term-tool-use","k":"Glossary","d":"When a model asks an app to run a tool, such as a search or a calculator, and then uses the result in its answer. Also called function calling.","x":""},{"t":"Training","u":"glossary/#term-training","k":"Glossary","d":"How an AI learns from its training data. It guesses, checks the answer and adjusts itself, millions of times.","x":""},{"t":"Training data","u":"glossary/#term-training-data","k":"Glossary","d":"The examples an AI learns from. How good and how varied they are shapes what the AI knows and how fair it is.","x":""},{"t":"Transformer","u":"glossary/#term-transformer","k":"Glossary","d":"The design behind almost all modern language models. Google researchers introduced it in 2017. It lets a model compare every word with every other word.","x":""},{"t":"Work product","u":"glossary/#term-work-product","k":"Glossary","d":"Materials prepared by or for a lawyer in anticipation of a lawsuit. They are usually protected from the other side in discovery.","x":""}];
